---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
const u = (p: string) => base + p.replace(/^\//, '');
---

<Layout title="About — Aruba Threat Radar" description="Why this initiative exists, how the intelligence is produced, and the standards it holds itself to." active="about">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,3.8rem);max-width:18ch;margin-top:18px">Local intelligence, published openly.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The Aruba Threat Radar is an independent, non-commercial initiative to give Aruba, Bonaire and Curaçao a
        consistent, structured and public picture of the threats that actually
        affect them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:10px">
    <div class="wrap article prose">
      <h2>Why this exists</h2>
      <p>
        When a local company suffers a breach, the public and its clients are often not told. There is no
        dedicated feed on the social platforms people actually use that explains, in plain language, what
        is circulating on the island and what to do about it. The result is a gap that attackers exploit:
        the same phishing email works on Aruba, Curaçao and Bonaire because nobody is connecting
        the dots in public.
      </p>
      <p>
        The July–August 2025 wave made the gap impossible to ignore. A court, a tax administration and a
        parliament were hit within days. Reporting was fragmented across Dutch, Papiamento and English
        outlets. There was no single place to see the timeline, the techniques and the indicators.
      </p>

      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A public threat radar.</strong> Live counts, latest reports, defanged indicators, feed health and a curated regional incident map.</li>
        <li><strong>A briefing service.</strong> Incident analysis and a weekly brief, with sources on every claim.</li>
        <li><strong>An open blueprint.</strong> The entire stack — compose files, connectors, collector, site, runbooks — is in the repository, so another island can copy it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What this is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Not a CERT.</strong> It does not have incident-response authority, and it cannot act on behalf of victims.</li>
        <li><strong>Not an attribution product.</strong> It will not name a group without public evidence. "Unknown" is an acceptable answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Not a vendor feed.</strong> There is no paywall, no lead-gen and no sponsored threat level.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Methodology</h2>
      <p>
        Intelligence is structured in <strong>OpenCTI</strong> using STIX 2.1. Public OSINT feeds are
        ingested through <strong>MISP</strong>; ATT&amp;CK, ATLAS, CISA KEV and reference data come in through
        official connectors. A collector queries the GraphQL API, applies a field allow-list, and writes a
        static JSON snapshot; the site renders that snapshot and never talks to the platform directly.
      </p>
      <p>
        The regional layer — the incident timeline, ATT&amp;CK coverage and briefings — is curated by hand
        from public reporting. Each entry links to its source. Where a report describes a technique but not
        a tool, the entry says so. Where attribution is absent, it stays absent.
      </p>

      <h2>Standards</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sourcing</td><td>Every factual claim in a briefing links to a public source. Corroboration is preferred over speed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Attribution</td><td>No naming of actors or countries without public evidence. Assessments are labelled as assessments.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Victims</td><td>No victim identities or personal data. Organisations are named only when they have named themselves publicly.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Handling</td><td>Public output is TLP:CLEAR. Anything else stays in the platform.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Corrections</td><td>Errors are corrected in public with a dated note, not silently edited.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact and corrections</h2>
      <p>
        The project lives at <a href="https://github.com/KevinFlorencio/opencti-aruba" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/KevinFlorencio/opencti-aruba</a>.
        Open an issue for corrections, source suggestions or reuse questions. For sensitive reports —
        a phishing sample, a local incident, a looked-over indicator — use the repository's private
        security advisory channel rather than a public issue.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you run an organisation on the island and want to contribute anonymised incident details, say so
        in the advisory. Victim identities are never published, and the source of a report is not disclosed
        without permission.
      </p>

      <h2>Licence</h2>
      <p>
        Written content is published under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a>.
        Code is MIT. Feed data remains under the licence of its origin, listed on the <a href={u('/sources/')}>sources page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Built with</h2>
      <p class="mono" style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--muted)">
        Proxmox VE · Debian 13 · Docker Compose · OpenCTI 7 · MISP · Elasticsearch · RabbitMQ · Redis ·
        Python · Astro · ECharts · Leaflet · Caddy
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
